% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 10 "Abduction", section 10.2 ("The grass is wet").
% Last verification: all tests PASS. The only harness notes are two
% "rule without variables" warnings: the book's grass-is-wet beliefs are
% genuinely propositional (no variables), so these warnings are expected and
% not errors. No failing tests.
%
% "it rained" and "the sprinkler was on" are open/assumable predicates,
% declared with "; unknown" so that querying "the grass is wet" returns them
% as alternative explanations (unknowns) instead of failing.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    the grass is wet.
    it rained; unknown.
    the sprinkler was on; unknown.

the knowledge base grass includes:

the grass is wet if it rained.
the grass is wet if the sprinkler was on.

scenario observed is:
    observe expects answers [
        "the grass is wet",
        "the grass is wet"
    ] and unknowns [
        "it rained",
        "the sprinkler was on"
    ].

query observe is:
    the grass is wet.
